@node LUA/ASSIGNMENT Statement
@section LUA ASSIGNMENT Statement

@cindex Lua assignment
@cindex Lua local
@cindex Lua global
@cindex Lua multiple assignment

@subheading Purpose

Assignment statements store values into variables, table fields, or other
storage locations. Lua supports both local and global variable assignment,
plus multiple simultaneous assignment.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@var{variable} = @var{expression}
local @var{variable} = @var{expression}
local @var{var1}, @var{var2}, @var{var3} = @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, @var{expr3}
@end example

Local variables are scoped to the current block. Global variables are visible
throughout the program. Multiple assignment allows tuple-like semantics.

@subheading Numeric Type Considerations

Lua supports numeric operations on all types. Assignment performs implicit
type conversion as needed:

@example
local score = 100              @r{BINARY-like number}
local price = 9999             @r{DECIMAL-like number}
local mixed = score + price    @r{arithmetic}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Assignment}
local score = 0
local lives = 3
score = score + 100
print(score)    @r{output: 100}

@r{Example 2: Local Variable}
function testLocal()
    local x = 10
    local y = 20
    return x + y
end
print(testLocal())  @r{output: 30}

@r{Example 3: Global Variable}
gameState = "running"
function updateState()
    gameState = "paused"
end
updateState()
print(gameState)  @r{output: paused}

@r{Example 4: Multiple Assignment}
local x, y, z = 1, 2, 3
print(x, y, z)  @r{output: 1 2 3}

@r{Example 5: Swap Assignment}
local a = 10
local b = 20
a, b = b, a      @r{swap values}
print(a, b)  @r{output: 20 10}
@end example

@subheading Table and Field Assignment

@example
local player = @{@}
player.x = 100
player.y = 50
player["health"] = 100

local items = @{@}
items[1] = "sword"
items[2] = "shield"
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Nil Assignment:} Assigning nil to a variable makes it undefined.
Accessing undefined variables returns nil.

@item @strong{Type Mismatch:} Assigning a value of one type to a variable of
another type works fine; Lua is dynamically typed.

@item @strong{Uninitialized Variables:} Accessing a variable before assignment
returns nil.

@item @strong{Excess Values:} Multiple assignment with more values than variables
ignores the extra values: @code{x, y = 1, 2, 3, 4} assigns 1 to x, 2 to y.

@item @strong{Insufficient Values:} Multiple assignment with fewer values than
variables assigns nil to remaining variables: @code{x, y, z = 1, 2} assigns z=nil.
@end itemize

@node LUA/IF-THEN-ELSE Statement
@section LUA IF-THEN-ELSE Statement

@cindex Lua if statement
@cindex Lua conditional
@cindex Lua elseif
@cindex Lua end keyword

@subheading Purpose

IF-THEN-ELSE statements conditionally execute code based on boolean expressions.
Lua supports simple if/then, if/then/else, and elseif chains.

@subheading Syntax

@example
if @var{condition} then
    @var{statements}
end

if @var{condition} then
    @var{statements}
else
    @var{statements}
end

if @var{cond1} then
    @var{stmts1}
elseif @var{cond2} then
    @var{stmts2}
elseif @var{cond3} then
    @var{stmts3}
else
    @var{stmts4}
end
@end example

@subheading Truth Values

In Lua, @code{nil} and @code{false} are falsy. All other values (0, "", @{@}, etc.)
are truthy.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple if}
local score = 150
if score > 100 then
    print("High Score!")
end

@r{Example 2: if-else}
local health = 0
if health > 0 then
    print("Alive")
else
    print("Dead")
end

@r{Example 3: elseif Chain}
local temperature = 25
if temperature > 30 then
    print("Hot")
elseif temperature > 15 then
    print("Warm")
elseif temperature > 0 then
    print("Cold")
else
    print("Freezing")
end

@r{Example 4: Nested if}
local x = 5
local y = 10
if x > 0 then
    if y > x then
        print("Y is greater")
    end
end

@r{Example 5: Complex Condition}
local score = 100
local lives = 3
local level = 1
if lives > 0 and score > 50 then
    if level <= 10 then
        print("Continue playing")
    end
end
@end example

@subheading Falsy vs Truthy

@example
if nil then print("Nil is true") end         @r{does nothing}
if false then print("False is true") end     @r{does nothing}
if 0 then print("Zero is true") end          @r{prints: Zero is true}
if "" then print("Empty string is true") end @r{prints: Empty string is true}
if @{@} then print("Empty table is true") end @r{prints: Empty table is true}
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Empty Condition:} Conditions must be valid expressions; invalid
syntax causes parse errors.

@item @strong{No else Required:} An if can exist without else. If the condition
is false, execution continues after the end.

@item @strong{Multiple elseifs:} Any number of elseif clauses can be chained;
only the first true condition executes.

@item @strong{Condition Side Effects:} If a condition is a function call, the
function executes even though only its boolean result matters.
@end itemize

@node LUA/WHILE Loop
@section LUA WHILE Loop (Pre-Test)

@cindex Lua while loop
@cindex Lua pre-test loop
@cindex Lua do-end

@subheading Purpose

WHILE loops repeatedly execute statements while a condition remains true.
The condition is tested before each iteration (pre-test).

@subheading Syntax

@example
while @var{condition} do
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

If the condition is false initially, the loop body never executes.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Counter}
local count = 1
while count <= 10 do
    print(count)
    count = count + 1
end

@r{Example 2: Decrement Loop}
local remaining = 100
while remaining > 0 do
    remaining = remaining - 10
    print(remaining)
end

@r{Example 3: Game Loop}
local running = true
while running do
    processEvents()
    updateGame()
    renderFrame()
    if quitRequested then
        running = false
    end
end

@r{Example 4: Infinite Loop with Break}
while true do
    local input = getInput()
    if input == "quit" then
        break
    end
    processInput(input)
end

@r{Example 5: Nested Loops}
local i = 1
while i <= 3 do
    local j = 1
    while j <= 3 do
        print(i, j)
        j = j + 1
    end
    i = i + 1
end
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Infinite Loop:} A condition that never becomes false causes
infinite execution; use break or modify condition inside loop.

@item @strong{Empty Body:} A while loop with no statements is allowed but
accomplishes nothing.

@item @strong{Modified Loop Variable:} Modifying the condition variable inside
the loop affects the next iteration's condition test.

@item @strong{Nested Loops:} Nested while loops are allowed; each break only
exits the innermost loop.
@end itemize

@node LUA/REPEAT-UNTIL Loop
@section LUA REPEAT-UNTIL Loop (Post-Test)

@cindex Lua repeat loop
@cindex Lua post-test loop
@cindex Lua until

@subheading Purpose

REPEAT-UNTIL loops execute a statement block, then test a condition. The body
always executes at least once (post-test). The loop exits when the condition
becomes true.

@subheading Syntax

@example
repeat
    @var{statements}
until @var{condition}
@end example

Note: UNTIL exits when condition is true (opposite of WHILE which continues
while condition is true).

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Password Prompt}
repeat
    print("Enter password:")
    local password = io.read()  @r{simplified}
    if password == "secret" then
        break
    end
until false  @r{or use explicit counter}

@r{Example 2: Numeric Loop}
local value = 1000
repeat
    print(value)
    value = value - 100
until value <= 0

@r{Example 3: Input Validation}
local input
repeat
    input = getInput()
until input ~= ""  @r{continue until non-empty}

@r{Example 4: Boundary Search}
local power = 1
repeat
    power = power * 2
until power > 1000
print(power)  @r{first power of 2 > 1000}

@r{Example 5: Guaranteed Execution}
local count = 0
repeat
    count = count + 1
    print("Execute at least once")
until count >= 1
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Guaranteed Execution:} Body executes at least once, even if
condition is initially true.

@item @strong{Condition Inversion:} UNTIL exits when true; WHILE continues
while true. Easy to confuse the logic.

@item @strong{Modified Condition Variable:} Changing condition variable inside
the loop affects the UNTIL test.

@item @strong{break Statement:} Can exit a repeat loop early via break.
@end itemize

@node LUA/FOR Numeric Loop
@section LUA FOR Numeric Loop (Counting Loop)

@cindex Lua for loop
@cindex Lua numeric for
@cindex Lua loop counter

@subheading Purpose

FOR loops iterate a fixed number of times using an explicit counter variable.
The loop variable automatically increments (or decrements) on each iteration.

@subheading Syntax

@example
for @var{var} = @var{start}, @var{end} do
    @var{statements}
end

for @var{var} = @var{start}, @var{end}, @var{step} do
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

The step defaults to 1 if omitted. Negative steps are supported for counting
downward.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Forward Loop}
for i = 1, 10 do
    print(i)
end

@r{Example 2: Counting Down}
for i = 10, 1, -1 do
    print(i)
end
print("Blastoff!")

@r{Example 3: Large Step}
for value = 0, 100, 5 do
    print(value)
end

@r{Example 4: Initialize Array}
local arr = @{@}
for i = 1, 10 do
    arr[i] = i * 10
end

@r{Example 5: Nested Loops}
for i = 1, 3 do
    for j = 1, 3 do
        print("(" .. i .. ", " .. j .. ")")
    end
end
@end example

@subheading Loop Variable Scope

The loop variable is local to the for loop body and is not accessible outside:

@example
for i = 1, 10 do
    print(i)
end
print(i)  @r{i is nil or previous value}
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Never-Executing Loop:} If start > end with positive step,
loop body never executes.

@item @strong{Zero Step:} Step of 0 causes infinite loop.

@item @strong{Non-Integer Step:} Fractional steps work but may cause
precision issues.

@item @strong{Overflow:} Loop variable near maximum may overflow on increment.

@item @strong{Modified Loop Variable:} Modifying the loop variable inside
the body causes unpredictable behavior on next iteration.
@end itemize

@node LUA/FOR-IN Iterator
@section LUA FOR-IN Iterator (Generic For)

@cindex Lua for-in loop
@cindex Lua generic for
@cindex Lua iterator

@subheading Purpose

FOR-IN loops iterate over the keys and values of tables using iterator functions.
This is Lua's generic iteration mechanism.

@subheading Syntax

@example
for @var{key} in pairs(@var{table}) do
    @var{statements}
end

for @var{index}, @var{value} in ipairs(@var{array}) do
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

@code{pairs()} iterates all key-value pairs; @code{ipairs()} iterates array
indices and values in order.

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Iterate Dictionary}
local player = @{name = "Hero", health = 100, x = 50, y = 50@}
for key in pairs(player) do
    print(key)
end

@r{Example 2: Iterate Array Elements}
local scores = @{100, 200, 150, 300@}
for index, value in ipairs(scores) do
    print("Score " .. index .. ": " .. value)
end

@r{Example 3: Sum Array Elements}
local values = @{10, 20, 30, 40@}
local sum = 0
for index, value in ipairs(values) do
    sum = sum + value
end
print("Sum: " .. sum)

@r{Example 4: Find Item in Array}
local items = @{"sword", "shield", "staff", "book"@}
for index, item in ipairs(items) do
    if item == "shield" then
        print("Found at index " .. index)
        break
    end
end

@r{Example 5: Modify Table During Iteration}
local data = @{a = 1, b = 2, c = 3@}
for key in pairs(data) do
    print(key .. ": " .. data[key])
    data[key] = data[key] * 2
end
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Modifying Table:} Modifying the table structure while iterating
(adding/removing keys) may cause undefined behavior.

@item @strong{Unordered Iteration:} @code{pairs()} iterates in undefined order;
use @code{ipairs()} for ordered iteration of array parts.

@item @strong{Sparse Arrays:} @code{ipairs()} stops at first nil; sparse arrays
may not iterate fully.

@item @strong{Multiple Loop Variables:} Both key and value are available in
the loop body.
@end itemize

@node LUA/FUNCTION Declaration
@section LUA FUNCTION Declaration

@cindex Lua function
@cindex Lua local function
@cindex Lua function definition

@subheading Purpose

Functions are reusable blocks of code that can be called multiple times.
Functions can take parameters and return values.

@subheading Syntax

@example
function @var{name}(@var{params})
    @var{statements}
end

local function @var{name}(@var{params})
    @var{statements}
end

@var{var} = function(@var{params})
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Simple Function}
function greet()
    print("Hello!")
end
greet()

@r{Example 2: Function with Parameters}
function add(a, b)
    return a + b
end
local result = add(5, 3)
print(result)  @r{output: 8}

@r{Example 3: Function with Multiple Returns}
function divmod(a, b)
    return math.floor(a / b), a % b
end
local quotient, remainder = divmod(17, 5)
print(quotient, remainder)  @r{output: 3 2}

@r{Example 4: Local Function}
function outer()
    local function inner()
        return "inner result"
    end
    return inner()
end

@r{Example 5: Anonymous Function}
local double = function(x)
    return x * 2
end
print(double(5))  @r{output: 10}
@end example

@subheading Variable Arguments

@example
function sum(...)
    local total = 0
    for i, v in ipairs(@{...@}) do
        total = total + v
    end
    return total
end
print(sum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5))  @r{output: 15}
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Extra Arguments:} Extra arguments passed to a function are ignored.

@item @strong{Missing Arguments:} Missing arguments are passed as nil.

@item @strong{No Return:} A function without explicit return returns nil.

@item @strong{Recursive Functions:} Functions can call themselves recursively;
stack overflow is possible with deep recursion.

@item @strong{Closures:} Functions capture variables from their enclosing scope,
creating closures.
@end itemize

@node LUA/LOCAL Declaration
@section LUA LOCAL Declaration (Local Variable)

@cindex Lua local
@cindex Lua variable scope
@cindex Lua local function
@cindex Lua local table

@subheading Purpose

LOCAL declarations create variables with block scope (limited to the current
block, function, or file). Local variables do not pollute the global namespace.

@subheading Syntax

@example
local @var{name}
local @var{name} = @var{expression}
local @var{name1}, @var{name2}, @var{name3}
local @var{name1}, @var{name2} = @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Local Variable}
function testLocal()
    local x = 10
    print(x)  @r{output: 10}
end
print(x)  @r{output: nil}

@r{Example 2: Multiple Local Variables}
local a, b, c = 1, 2, 3
print(a, b, c)  @r{output: 1 2 3}

@r{Example 3: Local Without Initialization}
local pending
if pending == nil then
    print("Uninitialized")
end

@r{Example 4: Local in Loop}
for i = 1, 3 do
    local message = "Iteration " .. i
    print(message)
end

@r{Example 5: Local Function}
local function helper(x)
    return x * 2
end
print(helper(5))  @r{output: 10}
@end example

@subheading Block Scope

@example
do
    local x = 10
    print(x)  @r{output: 10}
end
print(x)  @r{output: nil}
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Shadowing:} A local variable with the same name as an outer
scope variable shadows (hides) the outer variable.

@item @strong{Forward Reference:} A local can be referenced before its declaration
in the same scope; it will be nil until assigned.

@item @strong{Multiple Declaration:} Declaring the same local name twice in
the same scope is allowed; the second declaration shadows the first.
@end itemize

@node LUA/RETURN Statement
@section LUA RETURN Statement (Function Return)

@cindex Lua return
@cindex Lua return value
@cindex Lua multiple return

@subheading Purpose

RETURN statements exit a function, optionally returning one or more values
to the caller.

@subheading Syntax

@example
return
return @var{expression}
return @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, @var{...}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: No Return Value}
function doSomething()
    print("Working...")
    return
end

@r{Example 2: Single Return Value}
function getScore()
    return 1000
end
local score = getScore()
print(score)  @r{output: 1000}

@r{Example 3: Multiple Return Values}
function getMinMax(a, b, c)
    if a < b then
        if a < c then
            if b < c then
                return a, c
            else
                return a, b
            end
        else
            return c, b
        end
    else
        return b, c
    end
end
local min, max = getMinMax(10, 5, 20)
print(min, max)  @r{output: 5 20}

@r{Example 4: Early Return}
function findTarget(list, target)
    for i, v in ipairs(list) do
        if v == target then
            return i
        end
    end
    return nil
end

@r{Example 5: Conditional Return}
function divide(a, b)
    if b == 0 then
        return nil
    end
    return a / b
end
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{No Explicit Return:} Functions without explicit return return nil.

@item @strong{Multiple Returns:} Extra return values are discarded unless
captured by the caller.

@item @strong{Missing Assignments:} Multiple returns with fewer assignments
leave remaining variables nil.

@item @strong{Return in Nested Block:} Return exits the entire function,
not just the current block.
@end itemize

@node LUA/BREAK Statement
@section LUA BREAK Statement (Loop Exit)

@cindex Lua break
@cindex Lua loop exit
@cindex Lua early exit

@subheading Purpose

BREAK statements immediately exit the innermost enclosing loop (for, while, or repeat).

@subheading Syntax

@example
break
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Search Loop}
for i = 1, 100 do
    if items[i] == target then
        print("Found at " .. i)
        break
    end
end

@r{Example 2: Validation Loop}
local valid = false
repeat
    local input = getInput()
    if input == "quit" then
        break
    end
    if validateInput(input) then
        valid = true
    end
until valid

@r{Example 3: Nested Loop with Break}
for i = 1, 10 do
    for j = 1, 10 do
        if matrix[i][j] == 0 then
            print("Found at " .. i .. ", " .. j)
            break  @r{exits inner loop only}
        end
    end
end

@r{Example 4: Game Event Loop}
while gameRunning do
    local event = getEvent()
    if event == "quit" then
        break
    end
    processEvent(event)
end

@r{Example 5: Iterator with Early Exit}
for key, value in pairs(data) do
    if value > threshold then
        print("Threshold exceeded")
        break
    end
end
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Nested Loops:} break only exits the innermost loop; use flags or
returns to exit outer loops.

@item @strong{No Loop Context:} Using break outside a loop causes a syntax error.

@item @strong{Multiple Breaks:} Multiple break statements in different branches
of the same loop are allowed.
@end itemize

@node LUA/DO-END Block
@section LUA DO-END Block (Explicit Block)

@cindex Lua do-end
@cindex Lua block
@cindex Lua scope

@subheading Purpose

DO-END blocks create an explicit scope for local variables and statements.
Blocks are useful for limiting variable scope and organizing code.

@subheading Syntax

@example
do
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Local Scope}
do
    local x = 10
    local y = 20
    print(x + y)  @r{output: 30}
end
print(x)  @r{output: nil}

@r{Example 2: Nested Blocks}
do
    local outer = "outer"
    do
        local inner = "inner"
        print(outer)  @r{can access outer}
        print(inner)
    end
    print(inner)  @r{nil, out of scope}
end

@r{Example 3: Resource Management}
do
    local file = openFile("data.txt")
    local content = file:read()
    file:close()
    @r{file is out of scope after block}
end

@r{Example 4: Conditional Block}
do
    if gameDifficulty == "hard" then
        local hardMultiplier = 2
        enemyDamage = enemyDamage * hardMultiplier
    end
end

@r{Example 5: Multiple Blocks}
do
    local temp = 100
    temp = temp - 50
    print(temp)  @r{output: 50}
end
do
    local temp = 200
    print(temp)  @r{output: 200, different temp}
end
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Shadowing:} Variables in nested blocks can shadow outer variables.

@item @strong{Empty Block:} A do-end with no statements is allowed.

@item @strong{Nested Blocks:} Blocks can be arbitrarily nested.
@end itemize

@node LUA/TABLE Construction
@section LUA TABLE Construction (Table Literal)

@cindex Lua table
@cindex Lua array
@cindex Lua dictionary
@cindex Lua table literal

@subheading Purpose

TABLE construction creates table values (Lua's primary data structure).
Tables combine arrays, dictionaries, and objects into a single mechanism.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@var{table} = @{ @}
@var{array} = @{ @var{val1}, @var{val2}, @var{val3} @}
@var{dict} = @{ key1 = @var{val1}, key2 = @var{val2} @}
@var{mixed} = @{ 1, 2, name = "item", x = 10 @}
@end example

@subheading Compilable Examples

@example
@r{Example 1: Empty Table}
local empty = @{@}
print(#empty)  @r{output: 0}

@r{Example 2: Array Literal}
local scores = @{100, 200, 150, 300@}
for i, v in ipairs(scores) do
    print(v)
end

@r{Example 3: Dictionary Literal}
local player = @{
    name = "Hero",
    health = 100,
    x = 50,
    y = 50
@}
print(player.name)  @r{output: Hero}

@r{Example 4: Mixed Table}
local mixed = @{
    10, 20, 30,
    name = "Mixed",
    count = 3
@}
print(mixed[1])  @r{output: 10}
print(mixed.name)  @r{output: Mixed}

@r{Example 5: Nested Table}
local matrix = @{
    @{1, 0, 0@},
    @{0, 1, 0@},
    @{0, 0, 1@}
@}
print(matrix[2][2])  @r{output: 1}
@end example

@subheading Table Assignment

@example
local t = @{@}
t.x = 100
t["y"] = 200
t[1] = "first"
t[2] = "second"
@end example

@subheading Table Length

@example
local arr = @{10, 20, 30@}
print(#arr)  @r{output: 3}
table.insert(arr, 40)
print(#arr)  @r{output: 4}
@end example

@subheading Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Array vs Dictionary:} Arrays use numeric keys 1..N; dictionaries
use string or arbitrary keys.

@item @strong{Sparse Arrays:} Tables with gaps in numeric indices have undefined
length; #table only counts consecutive 1..N indices.

@item @strong{Table Identity:} Tables are compared by reference, not value.
Two tables with identical contents are not equal.

@item @strong{Mutable Structure:} Tables are mutable; modification affects all
references to the same table.

@item @strong{Nil Values:} Storing nil in a table removes the entry; table[key] = nil
deletes the key.
@end itemize

